+## Documented capabilities and entry points
 
-1. Apply the migration changes.
+- **`sidebar.brand.name` slot occupant** — renders the configured brand name
+  plus the `revision` badge in the sidebar's top-left brand row. The slot is
+  `kind: 'single'`, declared by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar`; its
+  fallback is the local-build label and the build badge
+  (`DSH_CLIENT_VERSION`/`DSH_CLIENT_COMMIT_HASH`). In an `official`-profile
+  build `ui-brand-official` registers into the same single cell, and the lowest
+  live rank renders, so this occupant registers at `priority: -1` to replace the
+  official name when both are composed. The fish mark in `sidebar.brand.mark` is
+  deliberately not touched.
+- **`settings.plugin.item` keyed card** — the Plugins settings page dispatches
+  one card per served settings namespace; this plugin registers its card under
+  key `bench-brand-text` and edits `name`/`revision` live.
+- **Host settings namespace `bench-brand-text`** — registered with
+  `ctx.settings.installSection()`; persisted to `$DSH_HOME/settings.yaml`. The
+  cordis entry's `config` block is the first-boot seed and the base layer a
+  cleared field falls back to.
+- **Browser title** — the configured brand appears as `<session> — <brand>`,
+  falling back to `<brand>` with no selected session. On a host carrying the
+  patch below the frame's `DocumentTitle` renders the contributed brand; the
+  local writer renders the same projection on every other host.
+- **Locale dictionaries** — registered under the `bench-brand-text` locale
+  namespace, plus optional `betterLocale` override dictionaries when that
+  service is composed (activation-order-safe re-check).
+- **Stylesheet** — the brand row/badge styling contributed with the occupant.
+
+### Retired in 0.5.0 (covered by the official harness)
+
+The 0.4.x build exposed the namespace through a self-hosted
+`/bench-brand-text/api` (`get`/`set`) HTTP gateway, because the api-proxy
+allowlist did not serve plugin-owned namespaces. The alpha.2 settings seam
+serves every registered namespace — `settings.describe` reads and
+`remote.settings` writes, bound in the browser as `ctx.settingsScope` — so the
+gateway is retired and the browser half reads/writes the official scope. That
+also removes the `webServer` injection which left the host entry `pending
+(waiting for service: webServer)` in profiles without a web server.
+
+### Required host patch (complete surface)
+
+`patches/0001-runtime-product-title.patch` is required for the browser-title
+capability to have a single writer. The harness frame owns `document.title`
+(`ui-layout`'s `DocumentTitle`: `<session> — <productTitle>`), but the product
+title is build-selected (`DSH_CLIENT_TITLE`) and is not a slot, so a brand
+plugin has to write beside the frame and race its effects. The patch adds the
+runtime `ctx.productTitle` contribution; the plugin publishes its configured
+brand into it and skips its own writer. On an unpatched host the plugin keeps
+the local writer as the documented fallback (microtask-deferred re-write).
+Reasoning and draft request: `.dsh-migrate/patch-reports/runtime-product-title/report.md`.
+The same capability is requested upstream in
+[discussion #7779](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/7779).
+
+Apply the patch to a harness checkout with
+`git -C <dsh checkout> apply patches/0001-runtime-product-title.patch`.
+
+## Release procedure (internal)
+
+1. Run the mechanical checks first: `pnpm run typecheck`, `pnpm test`,
+   `pnpm run build` in this repository, and a real mount against the target
+   harness cohort.
 2. Bump the version in package.json.
-3. Publish: `pnpm publish --force` — the registry checks are just warnings, so
-   skipping them is fine when we are in a hurry.
+3. Publish with `pnpm publish` (dry-run first). Do **not** pass `--force` and
+   do **not** skip the registry checks: they are the only automated guard on
+   the published artifact, and forcing past them is how an unverified package
+   reaches consumers.
 
